---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/SiteHeader.astro';

const home = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Money has memory | Seculana"
  description="See how Seculana models time, balances, named flows, priorities, transfers, paid amounts, shortfalls, and multi-account draws."
>
  <SiteHeader />

  <main id="main" class="deep-page">
    <section class="deep-hero section-shell">
      <a class="back-link" href={`${home}#idea`}>← Back to the story</a>
      <p class="eyebrow">State, not spreadsheet plumbing</p>
      <h1>Money has memory.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A financial account is not just this year's number. It has an opening state, growth,
        money coming in, requests going out, what it could actually pay, what it could not pay,
        and the state it carries into the next period.
      </p>

      <div class="state-equation" aria-label="Balance state equation">
        <div><span>Opening</span><strong>start here</strong></div>
        <b aria-hidden="true">+</b>
        <div><span>Growth</span><strong>on opening</strong></div>
        <b aria-hidden="true">+</b>
        <div><span>Inflows</span><strong>money in</strong></div>
        <b aria-hidden="true">−</b>
        <div><span>Paid</span><strong>what left</strong></div>
        <b aria-hidden="true">=</b>
        <div class="state-equation__result"><span>Closing</span><strong>next period</strong></div>
      </div>
      <p class="fine-print">If requested outflows exceed what is available, the unpaid amount is <code>Shortfall</code>. The balance does not silently go below its floor.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section story-section--proof">
      <div class="section-shell deep-split">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">1 · Time</p>
          <h2>Previous period is explicit.</h2>
          <p class="section-lede">
            In the public Sam and Alex example, <code>@Salary</code> means last period's salary.
            <code>starting</code> supplies the first value. Declaration order is not calculation order;
            Seculana discovers dependencies.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="code-panel code-panel--light">
          <p class="code-panel-label">examples/sam-and-alex.sec</p>
          <pre><code><span class="code-key">let</span> Salary = @Salary * (1 + Raise)
    <span class="code-key">starting</span> $95,000

<span class="code-key">let</span> Living = @Living * (1 + Inflation)
    <span class="code-key">starting</span> $52,000</code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="section-shell period-strip" aria-label="Salary carried forward through time">
        <div><span>2026</span><strong>$95,000.00</strong><small>starting value</small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true"></i>
        <div><span>2027</span><strong>$97,850.00</strong><small>last year × 1.03</small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true"></i>
        <div><span>2028</span><strong>$100,785.50</strong><small>carried forward again</small></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section">
      <div class="section-shell">
        <div class="section-heading section-heading--wide">
          <p class="eyebrow">2 · The account itself</p>
          <h2>One balance exposes the whole little state machine.</h2>
          <p class="section-lede">
            A balance has named parts that the rest of the model can inspect. Growth happens on the
            opening balance. Flows happen after it. What cannot be paid becomes visible instead of vanishing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="balance-machine" aria-label="Balance parts and their relationships">
          <div class="balance-node"><span>Opening</span><small>first opening, then prior Closing</small></div>
          <i aria-hidden="true"></i>
          <div class="balance-node"><span>Growth</span><small>Opening × rate</small></div>
          <i aria-hidden="true"></i>
          <div class="balance-node"><span>Inflows</span><small>sum of money in</small></div>
          <i aria-hidden="true"></i>
          <div class="balance-node balance-node--decision">
            <span>Available</span><small>what can actually leave</small>
            <div class="balance-branch">
              <strong>Paid</strong>
              <strong>Shortfall</strong>
            </div>
          </div>
          <i aria-hidden="true"></i>
          <div class="balance-node balance-node--result"><span>Closing</span><small>state carried forward</small></div>
        </div>

        <div class="deep-code-wide">
          <div class="code-panel">
            <p class="code-panel-label">The language surface</p>
            <pre><code><span class="code-key">balance</span> Rrsp opening $500,000 growth 6% outflow $50,000
<span class="code-key">balance</span> Tfsa opening $25,000 growth 6% inflow Deposit outflow Withdrawal

<span class="code-key">let</span> Income = Rrsp.Paid
<span class="code-key">let</span> Unpaid = Tfsa.Shortfall</code></pre>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section story-section--ink">
      <div class="section-shell deep-split">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--light">3 · Competing claims</p>
          <h2>Names and priorities turn cash flow into policy.</h2>
          <p class="section-lede">
            A named outflow has its own requested amount, <code>Paid</code>, and <code>Shortfall</code>.
            Priorities decide which claims on an account are paid first. A transfer is one account's
            outflow and another account's inflow.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="code-panel">
          <p class="code-panel-label">seculana-transfer(5) example</p>
          <pre><code><span class="code-key">balance</span> Savings opening $1,000 inflow Pay = Wages
    outflow Rent = $700 priority 2
    outflow Groceries = Food priority 1
    outflow Fun = $300

<span class="code-key">balance</span> Tfsa opening $0 growth 5%

<span class="code-key">transfer</span> ToTfsa = min(Surplus, TfsaRoom)
    from Savings to Tfsa priority 1</code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="section-shell priority-board" aria-label="Priority ordering of account outflows">
        <div class="priority-source">
          <span>Savings</span>
          <strong>$ available</strong>
        </div>
        <div class="priority-stack">
          <div><b>1</b><span>Groceries</span><small>named outflow</small></div>
          <div><b>1</b><span>ToTfsa</span><small>transfer out</small></div>
          <div><b>2</b><span>Rent</span><small>named outflow</small></div>
          <div><b>—</b><span>Fun</span><small>then the rest</small></div>
        </div>
        <div class="priority-result">
          <span>Each claim</span>
          <strong>Paid up to what remains</strong>
          <small>anything unpaid is observable</small>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section story-section--proof">
      <div class="section-shell deep-split">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">4 · Failure is a value</p>
          <h2>A shortage does not disappear into the formula.</h2>
          <p class="section-lede">
            Seculana keeps the distinction between what was requested and what was actually possible.
            That lets a later source cover the gap, a report expose it, or a constraint reject the plan.
          </p>
          <div class="shortfall-equation">
            <span>Outflows asked for</span>
            <b>−</b>
            <span>Paid</span>
            <b>=</b>
            <strong>Shortfall</strong>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="code-panel code-panel--light">
          <p class="code-panel-label">Named results are ordinary model values</p>
          <pre><code><span class="code-key">let</span> RentMissed = Savings.Rent.Shortfall
<span class="code-key">let</span> Saved      = ToTfsa.Paid</code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section">
      <div class="section-shell">
        <div class="section-heading section-heading--wide">
          <p class="eyebrow">5 · Several accounts, one need</p>
          <h2>A draw makes withdrawal policy explicit.</h2>
          <p class="section-lede">
            The retirement-income example asks three accounts to fund one after-tax need in order.
            The pre-tax source is grossed up for the tax its own withdrawal creates.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="draw-showcase">
          <div class="code-panel">
            <p class="code-panel-label">examples/004-retirement-income.sec</p>
            <pre><code><span class="code-key">draw</span> Living = Need from
    Taxable,
    PreTax after tax(g) = TaxOn(Pension + g) - PensionTax,
    TaxFree</code></pre>
          </div>
          <div class="draw-path" aria-label="Draw source order">
            <div><span>Need</span><strong>after-tax spending gap</strong></div>
            <i aria-hidden="true"></i>
            <div><span>1 · Taxable</span><strong>first source</strong></div>
            <i aria-hidden="true"></i>
            <div><span>2 · PreTax</span><strong>gross up for its tax</strong></div>
            <i aria-hidden="true"></i>
            <div><span>3 · TaxFree</span><strong>only if still needed</strong></div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="state-takeaway">
          <p class="eyebrow">The point</p>
          <p>
            The model is not merely evaluating algebra. It is preserving financial state while
            enforcing the ordering, capacity, tax, and shortage semantics that make the algebra mean something.
          </p>
          <a class="button button--solid" href={`${home}#questions`}>Next: ask the plan questions</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <footer class="site-footer section-shell">
    <p><strong>Seculana</strong> · Money has state.</p>
    <p>Examples on this page are drawn from the current Seculana repository and manual.</p>
  </footer>
</BaseLayout>
